[Applause]  mr. president mr. Provost madam  secretary college heads Dean colleagues  friends families and most of all women  and men of the class of 2017 welcome to  this day  [Applause]  Baccalaureate is one of the major  markers of your Yale journey and it  serves as the closing bookends to the  late summer of 2013 when you gathered in  the same space for your freshman  assembly baccalaureate invites you to  pause during what is an exhausting  weekend so please take a deep breath  slowly exhale and invest yourself in  this moment I now call on the university  chaplain  my sisters and brothers I invite you to  join me in the spirit of Prayer Almighty  and loving God you are the one who gives  us this day and all our days in the  majesty of this life moment we your  beloved children of different colors  Creed's generations stories and tongues  are gathered in this place as one human  family United in jubilant celebration we  are filled with gratitude for our  families friends teachers and guides  their arms have sheltered us their  shoulders have boosted us and today  their hearts store for us  eternal God it is you who calls us to  our full humanity as we celebrate our  accomplishment we are humbled and  overwhelmed by what lies ahead  let us continue to caress the beauty of  your creation and may our souls know  true delight as we awaken to the Wonder  and the mystery of our own unfolding  lives through unity of mind and spirit  hold us ever close as we seek to live in  your world wisely in the steadfast  pursuit of your life and your truth for  this we say Amen  all that which pervades earth sky and  heaven which is worthy of worship that  has no beginning that which is the light  of wisdom and truth let us meditate on  the radiance of that divinity made that  brilliance help inspire and illuminate  our mind that one which represents water  light and is the quintessence in all  things made that almighty spirit  pervading the earth atmosphere and  heaven bless us with enlightenment  send forth your light and your truth  they will lead me they will bring me to  your holy mountain to your dwelling  place that I may come to the altar of  God God my delight my joy that I may  praise you with the heart O God my God  you are the light of the world a city  built on a hill cannot be hid no one  after lighting a lamp puts it under the  bushel basket but on the lamp bed and it  gives light to all in the house in the  same way let your light shine before  others so that they may see your good  works and give glory to your God in  heaven God is the light of the heavens  and earth the likeness of his light is  that of Anish in which there is a lamp  the lamp is in a glass the glass is as  it were a brilliant star lit from a  blessed tree an olive trees neither of  the East nor of the West whose oil seems  to glow even no fire has touched it life  upon life God guides whom He wills to  his life God makes parables for Humanity  and God has knowledge of everything  [Applause]  [Music]  you  you  [Applause]  [Music]  on this occasion it is customary for the  president to deliver a baccalaureate  address and for the Dean of the old  college to offer a few readings four  years ago you came here as strangers but  now you leave here as friends think  about that think about how much you have  changed since you arrived think about  all the differences that define those  initial moments when you met your  suitemates  and thought maybe with some inward alarm  how is this going to work for an entire  year is he really going to wake up at  5:00 a.m. every day is she really going  to leave her laundry on the floor all  semester wow I never knew had such  strong feelings about country rock  classical electronica jazz trip-hop emo  when you think of back on those fragile  first days it is a miracle that a  community was born out of so much  difference it's impossible to know  precisely what makes that happen even in  hindsight but every year a new group of  implausibly different people from every  corner of the globe begins to turn into  friends for life  you will soon be leaving here with your  degrees but what you will take with you  into the world every day is the  community and the sense of community  that you have formed here I ask you to  join me for the next few minutes  thinking about that what makes a  community is a community made by its  memories for a thoughtful engagement  with these questions I turn to Elijah  Cooper the 1993 graduate of Yale College  who was in my disgust discussion section  when I was a graduate student and who  since then has written with grace about  a particular community his own family in  his book falling Cooper writes about his  two young daughters and how he wrestles  with the stresses and tensions and  transcendence --is that are part of  belonging to a family but the metaphor  easily extends to something as large and  complicated as a yale community he  writes daily rhythms create memories  walks to school breakfasts around the  kitchen table this is why I take my  daughters to cafes after school and we  drink hot chocolate and sit in the same  seats and look out the same window our  days accumulate painful memories like  seventh grade are unavoidable but maybe  our good memories can sit next to our  painful memories and watch over them  cushion them and soften their jagged  edges good memories hold us we create  them every day by walking out the door  or looking across the field  surely time and forgetfulness help too  but I'd like to think we are protected  by the memory of chocolate sometimes I  think we are memories I mean humans are  memories  we are the stories we tell and the  experiences we share in what must be the  greatest miracle of chemistry we are  able to enter into the memories of those  we love  we burrow into them drop down into their  cells in this way I hope I can nestle  into my children into the smallest part  of them and will always be with them  what makes a community  is a community made by its values you  came here expecting rightly to do to go  on to do great things for me it does not  matter what you do but how you do it go  to the daycare center the executive  suite the laboratory the equities market  the judicial chambers the Savannah  wherever you do the labor of the world  after you graduate invest yourself in it  but also commit yourselves to open  inquiry and understand that our fates  are linked in his new book on tyranny 20  lessons from the 20th century my  colleague and eminent historian Timothy  Snyder speaks to the importance of this  commitment the use of the lessons of the  past to remind us that our present and  future rests on a gossamer structure of  social interaction in which we are all  active participants I draw here from  three lessons first defend institutions  it is institutions that help us to  preserve decency they need our help as  well do not speak of our institutions  unless you make them yours by acting on  their behalf institutions do not protect  themselves they fall one after the other  unless each is defended from the  beginning so choose an institution you  care about a court a newspaper a law a  labor union and take its side second  believe in truth to abandon facts is to  abandon freedom if nothing is true then  no one can criticize power because there  is no basis upon which to do so if  nothing is true then all is spectacle  third make eye contact and small talk  this is not just polite it is part of  being a citizen and a responsible member  of society it is also a way to stay in  touch with your surroundings break down  social barriers and understand whom you  should and should not trust if we enter  a culture of denunciation you will want  to know the psychological landscape of  your daily life what makes a community  is a community made up of the  unacknowledged through her poetry  Lucille Clifton asks us to broaden our  understanding of who can be an expert of  who can be a vital contributing member  of our society of who in fact helps  define a community as I read her poem  think about the dining hall workers the  custodians the grounds crew and the  administrative assistants you have seen  or even better come to know during your  Yale career think about how they have  made your life easier more complete and  more meaningful recognize their  expertise and labor remember how they  used their skills to support you thank  them study the masters like my aunt  Timmy it was her iron ore one like hers  that smooths the sheets the master poet  slept on home or hotel what matters is  he lay himself down on her handiwork and  dreamed she dreamed two words some  Cherokee some Maasai and some huge and  particular as hope if you had heard her  chanting a she ironed you would  understand form and line and discipline  and order and America  what makes a community it is made up of  memories values and the unacknowledged  it is also made up of you when I look at  you I see many things love contestation  ideology zaniness pride brilliance  insecurity creativity psets essay  deadlines wenzels more p-sets  loathes chicken tenders and kate shark  as you leave you'll have to explain it  to your parents as you leave this place  as you leave this place remember these  things remember what you have given to  this community and it to you remember  the transcendent as well as the  chocolate remember to articulate and  defend your values remember to  acknowledge the work and skill and care  of those who labor invisibly on your  behalf  remember to understand your role in  helping this and future communities  realize their potential most of all  remember the story of your community  wherever you make it and then tell  others what you see it is only through  the telling of these stories that we can  ever help our communities become the  light that is in the truth I leave you  with an excerpt from Frank O'Hara's  having a coke with you I look at you and  I would rather look at you that all the  portraits in the world except possibly  for the polish writer occasionally and  anyway it's in the Frick which thank  heavens you haven't gone to yet so we  can go together for the first time and  the fact that you move so beautifully  more or less take care of futurism just  as at home I never think of the nude  descending a staircase or at a rehearsal  a single drawing of Leonardo  Michelangelo that used to WoW me and  what good does all the research of the  Impressionists do them when they never  got the right person to stand near the  tree when the Sun sank or for that  matter Marino Marini when he didn't pick  the rider as carefully as the horse it  seems they were all cheated of some  marvelous experience which is not going  to go wasted on me which is why I'm  telling you about it  women and men of the class of 2017 it  has been an honor and a privilege to be  a service to your community take care of  it take care of each other and take care  of yourselves thank you and  congratulations  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  before I begin class of 2017 guests  families members might do join me in  thanking Dean Holloway for his service  to Yale and wishing him luck as the next  Provost of Northwestern University  graduates of the class of 2017 family  members friends it's so good to be with  here with you today  a day filled with joy and hope and I'm  sure a bit of pride as well  there's a wonderful Yale tradition that  I'd like to honor right now may I ask  all of the families and friends here  today to rise and recognize the  outstanding and graduating members of  the class of 2017  and now class of 2017 may I ask you to  consider all those who have supported  your arrival at this milestone and to  please rise and recognize them  [Applause]  so last month I flew to California to  celebrate Passover with my family as  many of you know this is the holiday  that memorializes the experiences of  Jews as slaves in Egypt and then  celebrates their deliverance from  bondage through reading a liturgical  texts called the Hagaddah  and eating ritual foods everyone at the  Passover Seder is invited to reflect on  this history as if they had experienced  it themselves the Passover themes of  slavery and freedom resonate as clearly  today as they did millennia ago  many contemporary haga don't reference  modern struggles such as African  Americans fight for emancipation and  civil rights and present-day efforts to  end human trafficking my family uses the  traditional Haggadah sponsored by  Maxwell House coffee since 1932  apparently it was also used by former  President Obama at the White House's  annual seder my favorite passage comes  early in the evening we are reminded  that because we were strangers in the  land of Egypt we therefore must welcome  strangers to our own Seder we recite let  all those who are hungry enter and eat  all who are in distress come and  celebrate Passover with us these  sentiments go a long way back they're  written in Aramaic the everyday spoken  language of must of the Middle East from  2,000 to 3,000 years ago so that they  could be easily understood by those who  might have responded to the offer in the  past the head of the household  open the door open the door to the home  and read these words inviting all who  past to come and join in the celebration  remembering what it was like to be a  stranger is not only important at  Passover  we're told repeatedly in the Jewish  scripture to show kindness toward  strangers the Torah warns against  harming a stranger anywhere from thirty  five to forty six distinct times  depending on how you translate it in  Exodus we are told you shall not wronged  a stranger or oppress him for you were  strangers in the land of Egypt elsewhere  in Exodus this is written for you were  strangers in a strange land that's the  inspiration for the title of the popular  science fiction novel by Robert Heinlein  and again in leviticus we read when a  stranger resides with you in your land  you shall not do him wrong the stranger  who resides with you shall be to you as  the native among you and you shall love  him as yourself for you were aliens in  the land of Egypt Judea knows Judaism is  not alone in this concern for the  stranger in the parable of the Last  Judgement Jesus says I was a foreigner  and you welcomed me in his  interpretation of this passage the  bishop emeritus of the Archdiocese of  Hartford  Peter Rosa says that welcoming the  foreigner  welcoming the foreigner is equivalent to  welcoming Jesus later in the Epistle to  the Hebrews we were advised do not  neglect to show hospitality to strangers  for by this some have entertained angels  without knowing it imagine if all  strangers all foreigners were treated as  if they were divine messengers  the Koran instructs Muslims similarly  and do good unto your parents and near  of kin and unto orphans and the needy  and the neighbor from among your own  people and the neighbor who is a  stranger and the friend by your side and  the wayfarer there's an ancient Hindu  scripture that says the guest is  equivalent to God hospitality to guests  and strangers called manushya yagna is  one of the five great sacrifices to be  carried out daily according to Vedic  practice turning back to the Old  Testament we find this reminder worded a  bit differently from the others  you shall not oppress a stranger as you  yourself know the feelings of a stranger  for you were strangers in the land of  Egypt this is also translated quite  beautifully as for you know the heart of  a stranger okay psychologists and I'm a  psychologist called this kind of  understanding knowing the heart of  another empathy and I've linked it to  acting ethically and morally we're more  likely to treat other people well if we  can find ways to empathize with them  here we are told how to act how not to  mistreat strangers because we can  understand their feelings their hearts  but is it easy to show empathy not  always this is one reason why no group  no religion has ever completely lived up  to its own ideals regarding the  treatment of outsiders intolerance and  oppression have been a ubiquitous  feature of many human societies when we  are feeling lost threat or fear it is  hard for us to put ourselves in someone  else's shoes  in fact stressful and uncertain times  may lead us to point fingers at others  to search for convenient scapegoats  rather than empathize with them today it  is not only the immigrants and refugees  among us who are strangers in a strange  land across the United States and even  across the world we have all become  strangers to each other  we find it difficult to understand and  engage perspectives with which we  disagree we can't imagine how people in  our own country can feel and act as they  do and despite being in constant  communication we feel increasingly  disconnected from one another in the  last thirty years according to a recent  article in Forbes the number of  Americans who report that they have no  friends zero has tripled how can this be  how can this be when we can friend  hundreds of individuals on social media  with the tap of a finger yet the more  one's life is oriented around the  Internet the lonelier one tends to be  it's easier to email text and tweet than  to put away our smartphones and make a  new friend with a living breathing human  this is not a new phenomenon and the  Internet may actually attract more than  its fair share of blame at the turn of  the 20th century the French sociologist  Emile Durkheim  wrote about the emergence of anomie a  sense of being unmoored or unconnected  to social norms and institutions  he thought that great changes in his  lifetime  the rise of industry mass production the  growth of cities were responsible for  these feelings of disconnect essentially  later but a few years before concerns  about the internet and social media  started to shape this conversation  political scientist Robert Putnam used  the vivid metaphor of Bowling Alone to  describe the general breakdown of true  personal social networks in contemporary  society Putnam found that we are no  longer a community of joiners we no  longer define ourselves by clubs and  civic organizations that represent our  hobbies and our interests so what  happens when social ties weaken how do  we feel secure when we are no longer  embedded deeply in social networks or  community groups one possible outcome is  that we retreat into ourselves we may  spend a lot of time staring into the  mirror attending only to information  that is consistent with our pre-existing  beliefs we focus on our own doubts our  own vulnerabilities our own  preoccupations we've become isolated we  become lonely we have all known the long  loneliness wrote Dorothy day and we have  learned that the only solution is love  and that love comes with community as  our Dean just told us without community  without empathy we cannot see ourselves  in the eyes of the strangers among us  instead those strangers are the best  ignored and at worst demonized and  despite the teachings of Hinduism Islam  Christianity Judaism and many other  traditions to the contrary we no longer  welcome the strangers to our table  rather we build walls to keep them away  from us perhaps on a beautiful sunny  afternoon in the last four years you  took a walk over to the Grove Street  cemetery and you found the tombstone of  Kingman Brewster yells 17th president  Brewster let's University during a  tumultuous period in its history and in  the nation's history he oversaw Co  education and tried to keep the peace  while the black pants  trials were held in New Haven Brewster's  epitaph reads the presumption of  innocence is not only a legal concept in  common law and in common sense it  requires a generosity of spirit a  generosity of spirit toward the stranger  the expectation of what is best rather  than what is worst in each other  Brewster was familiar with conflict  he knew how deeply people could disagree  with one another how fear and  misunderstanding could divide them yet  he expected the best in others that was  his common sense  let it be ours too empathy is a powerful  tool Tana has eight coats the writer and  social critic has called for a muscular  empathy rooted in curiosity such a  muscular empathy should inspire us and  spur us to action to serve others and  our communities members of the class of  2017 you've just spent four years on a  campus that strives to be welcoming and  inclusive you've made lasting  friendships with people from a variety  of backgrounds and walks of life  although you are strangers to each other  when you met together you helped build a  strong community here at Yale you have  all stood up and saying the seasons come  the seasons go the earth is green or  white with snow but time and change  shall not avail to break the friendships  formed at Yale  I  I urge you I urge you to take these  experiences out into the world a world  that desperately needs your service your  curiosity and yes your empathy I hope  you identify with the plight of the  other walking a mile in the shoes of a  stranger by welcoming guests by doing  good to strangers by knowing the hearts  of others you may well entertain angels  members of the class of 2017 please rise  as you go on to a world that is all  before you hand-in-hand with wandering  steps and slow bring to that world all  that your Yale education has given you  the ability to appreciate complexity  even while seeking simplicity to engage  critically even while listening  respectfully to recognize your  responsibilities while finding pleasures  in your new communities and to welcome  into these communities the strangers  among us we are delighted to salute your  accomplishments and we are proud of your  achievements  remember to give thanks for all that has  brought you to this day and go forth  from this place with grateful hearts  paying back the gifts you have received  here by using your minds your voices and  your hands to strengthen your new  communities and your world  congratulations class of 2017  [Applause]  [Music]  you  Oh  ah  [Music]  you  may God bless you and keep you may the  eternal one show you favor and be  gracious to you may the Holy One cause  light to shine upon you and grant you  peace and let us say Amen  [Music]  you  [Music]

